Chapter: DtlsMessage
  cites Foreword chapter Tls
  cites Foreword chapter Hmac
  cites Foreword chapter Hkdf
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256

 The DTLS 1.3 handshake message layer (RFC 9147 sections 5.2 and 7).
 Phase D2b: the framing the D2a machine sequences but does not encode.

 THE TRANSCRIPT RULE. RFC 9147 section 5.2, and it is worth quoting
 because getting it wrong produces a Finished that verifies against
 itself and against no peer on earth: the message transcript is computed
 over the original TLS 1.3-style Handshake messages WITHOUT the
 message_seq, fragment_offset, and fragment_length values. So a DTLS
 handshake message goes on the wire with a 12-byte header and into the
 transcript with a 4-byte one. Two shapes of the same message, and an
 implementation that hashes what it sent is wrong. tls13-msg-transcript
 is the only correct way to feed a message to the hash; the wire form
 must never reach it.

 THE FINISHED. RFC 8446 section 4.4.4, inherited whole. The finished key
 is HKDF-Expand-Label(base-secret, "finished", "", 32) -- an EMPTY
 context, not the transcript -- and the verify data is an HMAC of that
 key over the transcript hash. The two are easy to transpose and the
 result looks like a key either way.

 THE ACK. RFC 9147 section 7. A list of record numbers, each an epoch
 and a sequence number, saying what actually arrived. It is what lets a
 flight stop being retransmitted.

 We say:

Section: Handshake Types

 RFC 9147 section 5.2. HelloRetryRequest is not in this list: DTLS 1.3
 reuses TLS 1.3's, which is a ServerHello carrying a special random and
 the cookie extension -- not the separate HelloVerifyRequest that DTLS
 1.2 had. A parser that looks for a distinct HRR type will never find one.

  dtls-hs-client-hello : Integer = 1
  dtls-hs-server-hello : Integer = 2
  dtls-hs-encrypted-ext : Integer = 8
  dtls-hs-certificate : Integer = 11
  dtls-hs-cert-verify : Integer = 15
  dtls-hs-finished : Integer = 20

 RFC 9147 section 7: the ACK is its own record content type, 26.

  dtls-content-ack : Integer = 26

Section: Types

  DtlsMsg = record {
    dm-type : Integer,
    dm-length : Integer,
    dm-seq : Integer,
    dm-frag-offset : Integer,
    dm-frag-length : Integer,
    dm-body : List Integer
  }

  DtlsRecordNum = record {
    rn-epoch : Integer,
    rn-seq : Integer
  }

Section: Framing

 The wire form: msg_type, uint24 length, uint16 message_seq, uint24
 fragment_offset, uint24 fragment_length, body. Twelve bytes of header.

  dtls-msg-encode : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-msg-encode (msg-type) (message-seq) (body) =
    let n = list-length body
    in [msg-type] & dtls-be24 n & dtls-be16 message-seq & dtls-be24 0 & dtls-be24 n & body

 A fragment carries the length of the WHOLE message, not of its own piece:
 the uint24 length field is the message and fragment_length is the piece.
 Emitting the piece's size in both is the mistake that makes a receiver
 believe the first fragment is a complete short message, and it is invisible
 against a receiver that reassembles the same way.

  dtls-msg-encode-frag : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-msg-encode-frag (msg-type) (message-seq) (total-len) (frag-offset) (frag-body) =
    [msg-type] & dtls-be24 total-len & dtls-be16 message-seq & dtls-be24 frag-offset & dtls-be24 (list-length frag-body) & frag-body

 A body that fits in one fragment produces exactly one fragment, and it is
 byte-identical to dtls-msg-encode's output, so fragmenting is never a
 behaviour change for a message that did not need it.

  dtls-msg-fragments : Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List (List Integer)
  dtls-msg-fragments (msg-type) (message-seq) (body) (max-body) =
    if max-body <= 0 then [dtls-msg-encode msg-type message-seq body]
    else dtls-frag-loop msg-type message-seq body max-body 0 []

  dtls-frag-loop : Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, List (List Integer) -> List (List Integer)
  dtls-frag-loop (msg-type) (message-seq) (body) (max-body) (off) (acc) =
    let n = list-length body
    in if off >= n & list-length acc > 0 then acc
    else let take = if n - off > max-body then max-body else n - off
    in let piece = dtls-slice-at body off take
    in dtls-frag-loop msg-type message-seq body max-body (off + take) (list-push acc (dtls-msg-encode-frag msg-type message-seq n off piece))

 The transcript form. RFC 9147 section 5.2: no message_seq, no
 fragment_offset, no fragment_length. What is left is exactly the TLS 1.3
 shape, so the function that builds it is tls13-msg-transcript in
 Foreword chapter Tls and this chapter's job is only to strip the DTLS
 fields back off before hashing what the peer sent.

  dtls-msg-to-transcript : DtlsMsg -> List Integer
  dtls-msg-to-transcript (m) = tls13-msg-transcript (m.dm-type) (m.dm-body)

  dtls-msg-parse : List Integer -> Maybe DtlsMsg
  dtls-msg-parse (bs) =
    if list-length bs < 12 then None
    else dtls-msg-parse-body bs

  dtls-msg-parse-body : List Integer -> Maybe DtlsMsg
  dtls-msg-parse-body (bs) =
    let len = dtls-rd24 bs 1
    in let seq = dtls-rd16 bs 4
    in let foff = dtls-rd24 bs 6
    in let flen = dtls-rd24 bs 9
    in if 12 + flen > list-length bs then None
    else Just (DtlsMsg { dm-type = list-at bs 0, dm-length = len, dm-seq = seq, dm-frag-offset = foff, dm-frag-length = flen, dm-body = dtls-slice-at bs 12 flen })

Section: Reassembly

 RFC 9147 section 5.2 requires a receiver to accept a message split across
 datagrams, in any order, and to tolerate a peer that refragments the same
 message differently when it retransmits the flight. Both follow from
 flights being retransmitted whole: the second copy of a message is not
 obliged to be cut where the first one was, so fragments overlap and repeat
 as a matter of course rather than as an attack.

 The bounds are attacker-facing rather than tidy-minded. A peer we have not
 authenticated yet chooses the declared length and the fragment count, so
 both are refused past a ceiling instead of being allocated for. A
 handshake message over 16384 bytes is refused outright; sixteen fragments
 is the retained maximum.

  dtls-reasm-max-frags : Integer = 16
  dtls-reasm-max-len : Integer = 16384

  DtlsFrag = record {
    df-offset : Integer,
    df-bytes : List Integer
  }

  DtlsReasm = record {
    dr-active : Boolean,
    dr-type : Integer,
    dr-seq : Integer,
    dr-length : Integer,
    dr-frags : List DtlsFrag
  }

  dtls-reasm-new : DtlsReasm
  dtls-reasm-new = DtlsReasm { dr-active = False, dr-type = 0, dr-seq = 0, dr-length = 0, dr-frags = [] }

  dtls-reasm-start : DtlsMsg -> DtlsReasm
  dtls-reasm-start (m) =
    DtlsReasm { dr-active = True, dr-type = m.dm-type, dr-seq = m.dm-seq, dr-length = m.dm-length, dr-frags = [] }

 A fragment for a different message_seq is not an error and must not be
 dropped: it is the next message of the flight arriving while the previous
 one is still incomplete. The buffer restarts on it rather than refusing it,
 which is the behaviour that lets a flight make progress when its first
 message was lost outright.

  dtls-reasm-add : DtlsReasm, DtlsMsg -> DtlsReasm
  dtls-reasm-add (r) (m) =
    if m.dm-length > dtls-reasm-max-len then r
    else if m.dm-frag-offset + m.dm-frag-length > m.dm-length then r
    else if list-length (m.dm-body) /= m.dm-frag-length then r
    else let base = if r.dr-active & r.dr-seq == m.dm-seq & r.dr-type == m.dm-type then r else dtls-reasm-start m
    in if list-length (base.dr-frags) >= dtls-reasm-max-frags then base
    else DtlsReasm { dr-active = True, dr-type = base.dr-type, dr-seq = base.dr-seq, dr-length = base.dr-length, dr-frags = list-push (base.dr-frags) (DtlsFrag { df-offset = m.dm-frag-offset, df-bytes = m.dm-body }) }

 Coverage is the furthest point reachable from zero by repeatedly taking any
 fragment that starts at or before it. Recomputing from scratch each pass is
 what makes an overlapping or duplicated fragment harmless: the answer
 depends on the union of the fragments and not on the order they arrived.
 At most sixteen passes over sixteen entries.

  dtls-reasm-covered : DtlsReasm -> Integer
  dtls-reasm-covered (r) = dtls-cov-rounds (r.dr-frags) 0 0

  dtls-cov-rounds : List DtlsFrag, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dtls-cov-rounds (fs) (covered) (round) =
    if round >= dtls-reasm-max-frags then covered
    else let next = dtls-cov-pass fs covered 0
    in if next <= covered then covered
    else dtls-cov-rounds fs next (round + 1)

  dtls-cov-pass : List DtlsFrag, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dtls-cov-pass (fs) (covered) (i) =
    if i >= list-length fs then covered
    else let f = list-at fs i
    in let f-end = f.df-offset + list-length (f.df-bytes)
    in let c2 = if f.df-offset <= covered & f-end > covered then f-end else covered
    in dtls-cov-pass fs c2 (i + 1)

  dtls-reasm-complete : DtlsReasm -> Boolean
  dtls-reasm-complete (r) = r.dr-active & dtls-reasm-covered r >= r.dr-length

  dtls-reasm-body : DtlsReasm -> List Integer
  dtls-reasm-body (r) = dtls-asm-loop (r.dr-frags) (r.dr-length) 0 []

  dtls-asm-loop : List DtlsFrag, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-asm-loop (fs) (total) (pos) (acc) =
    if pos >= total then acc
    else let pick = dtls-asm-find fs pos 0 (-1)
    in if pick < 0 then acc
    else let f = list-at fs pick
    in let f-end = f.df-offset + list-length (f.df-bytes)
    in let take = f-end - pos
    in dtls-asm-loop fs total f-end (acc & dtls-slice-at (f.df-bytes) (pos - f.df-offset) take)

  dtls-asm-find : List DtlsFrag, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dtls-asm-find (fs) (pos) (i) (best) =
    if i >= list-length fs then best
    else let f = list-at fs i
    in let f-end = f.df-offset + list-length (f.df-bytes)
    in let ok = f.df-offset <= pos & f-end > pos
    in let better = if best < 0 then True else f-end > (list-at fs best).df-offset + list-length ((list-at fs best).df-bytes)
    in dtls-asm-find fs pos (i + 1) (if ok & better then i else best)

  dtls-reasm-message : DtlsReasm -> Maybe DtlsMsg
  dtls-reasm-message (r) =
    if dtls-reasm-complete r == False then None
    else Just (DtlsMsg { dm-type = r.dr-type, dm-length = r.dr-length, dm-seq = r.dr-seq, dm-frag-offset = 0, dm-frag-length = r.dr-length, dm-body = dtls-slice-at (dtls-reasm-body r) 0 (r.dr-length) })

Section: Finished

 The Finished itself is RFC 8446 section 4.4.4, which DTLS 1.3 inherits
 unchanged, so the key, the verify data and the constant-time check are
 tls13-finished-key, tls13-verify-data and tls13-finished-ok in Foreword
 chapter Tls. What is DTLS here is only the framing: a Finished has to go
 out as a DTLS handshake message, with a message_seq.

  dtls-finished-msg : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-finished-msg (base-secret) (transcript-hash) (message-seq) =
    dtls-msg-encode dtls-hs-finished message-seq (tls13-verify-data base-secret transcript-hash)

Section: ACK

 RFC 9147 section 7. A vector of record numbers, each a uint64 epoch and
 a uint64 sequence number, preceded by its length in BYTES. The ACK says
 what arrived, which is what lets the peer stop retransmitting a flight
 it has already delivered.

  dtls-ack-encode : List DtlsRecordNum -> List Integer
  dtls-ack-encode (rns) =
    let body = dtls-ack-body rns 0 (list-length rns) []
    in dtls-be16 (list-length body) & body

  dtls-ack-body : List DtlsRecordNum, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ack-body (rns) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let r = list-at rns i
    in dtls-ack-body rns (i + 1) n (acc & dtls-be64 (r.rn-epoch) & dtls-be64 (r.rn-seq))

  dtls-ack-count : List Integer -> Integer
  dtls-ack-count (bs) =
    if list-length bs < 2 then 0
    else dtls-rd16 bs 0 / 16

Section: Helpers

  dtls-be16 : Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-be16 (v) = [bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and v 255]

  dtls-be24 : Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-be24 (v) = [bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and v 255]

  dtls-be64 : Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-be64 (v) =
    [bit-and (bit-shru v 56) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 48) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 40) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 32) 255,
     bit-and (bit-shru v 24) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and v 255]

  dtls-rd16 : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dtls-rd16 (bs) (off) = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at bs off) 8) (list-at bs (off + 1))

  dtls-rd24 : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dtls-rd24 (bs) (off) =
    let a = bit-shl (list-at bs off) 16
    in let b = bit-shl (list-at bs (off + 1)) 8
    in bit-or a (bit-or b (list-at bs (off + 2)))

  dtls-slice-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-slice-at (bs) (off) (n) = dtlsm-slice-loop bs off n 0 []

  dtlsm-slice-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtlsm-slice-loop (bs) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length bs then acc
    else dtlsm-slice-loop bs off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs (off + i)))
